# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source files are stored and checked out with LF on every platform. Without
# this, a clone on Windows gets CRLF, the determinism test compares bytes and
# fails, and the failure looks like a code defect rather than a checkout
# setting.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files whose bytes must survive untouched
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This tool deliberately produces files with mixed CRLF and LF, with CR only,
# with a byte order mark where none is expected and with no final newline.
# Those are corruption mutators at layer L0, so the fixtures that carry them
# are not text to be normalised. They are exact bytes under test.
#
# Marking them -text also means git diff reports them as binary, which is the
# correct trade here. A diff that silently hides a line ending change is worse
# than no diff at all for these files.
#
# The pattern reaches every testdata directory, not only the one at the root.
# Measured: testdata/x was protected and internal/domain/seed/testdata/x was
# not, because a pattern containing a slash is anchored where this file lives.
# Go puts fixtures next to the package that owns them, so the anchored form
# would have left most of them unprotected.
**/testdata/** -text
*.golden -text

# The module carried in third_party is held byte for byte to what its author
# published, and a guard compares the two. Normalising its line endings would
# be a change to somebody else's file that no patch describes.
third_party/** -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated and vendored content, kept out of language statistics
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
spec/*.yaml linguist-language=YAML
